different (p = 0.46). Choosing this way holds up on later windows (within under 0.1%).","synthetic:random_walk: Choose naive. By mase it is the best model with probability 0.67; counting the cases where another model is better, picking it costs on average 0.2% more than picking the best would. naive and seasonal_naive give identical forecasts here. Backtests flatter the winner: choosing this way scores 2.36 on later windows, not 2.34 (0.6% worse).","synthetic:rossler: Choose seasonal_naive. By mase it is the best model with probability 1.00, in every resample of your series. seasonal_naive beats mean (p = 0.000274, confirmed by simulating equally good forecasters on your data). Choosing this way holds up on later windows (within under 0.1%).","synthetic:seasonal_trend: Choose seasonal_naive. By mase it is the best model with probability 1.00, in every resample of your series. seasonal_naive beats damped_drift (p = 0.000867, confirmed by simulating equally good forecasters on your data). Choosing this way holds up on later windows (within under 0.1%).","synthetic:van_der_pol: Choose seasonal_naive. By mase it is the best model with probability 1.00, in every resample of your series. seasonal_naive beats mean (p = 5.12e-10, confirmed by simulating equally good forecasters on your data). Choosing this way holds up on later windows (within under 0.1%).","(all): Choose seasonal_naive. By mase it is the best model with probability 1.00, in every resample of your series. seasonal_naive beats naive (p = 0.0017, confirmed by simulating equally good forecasters on your data). Choosing this way holds up on later windows (within under 0.1%).","One model for everything is enough: choosing per segment (synthetic:ar_differences: naive, synthetic:lorenz: mean, synthetic:random_walk: naive, synthetic:rossler: seasonal_naive, synthetic:seasonal_trend: seasonal_naive, synthetic:van_der_pol: seasonal_naive) changes the score by 0.5% on later windows, which is not a reliable gain (p = 0